# Remit self-host deployment configuration (RFC 035 D6).
#
# This is the whole configuration surface: every service reads it via
# docker-compose.sqlite.yml's `env_file: .env`. Copy this file to `.env` next to
# docker-compose.sqlite.yml, fill in every value marked SECRET below with your
# own, then bring the stack up.
#
#   cp remit.env.template .env
#   chmod 600 .env       # secrets live in this file — keep it private
#   $EDITOR .env
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.sqlite.yml --env-file .env up -d
#
# Compose does not auto-discover a file named docker-compose.sqlite.yml, so -f
# is not optional here. `remit restart` is the same command with those flags
# already filled in.
#
# Nothing here is a real secret as shipped; every SECRET value below MUST be
# replaced before this stack touches real mail.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image pin (RFC 035 D3/D8)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# `latest` tracks main and is what makes the quickstart copy-pasteable; it
# is a moving pointer, not a release. Once you have run this once, pin a
# specific build: find the tag at
# https://github.com/remit-mail/reader/pkgs/container/reader%2Fbackend (every
# image in the roster shares a tag — they are built and tagged together) and
# set REMIT_TAG=sha-<git-sha>, or better, pin by digest
# (ghcr.io/remit-mail/reader/backend@sha256:...) directly in
# docker-compose.sqlite.yml if you want to survive a tag being force-moved.
REMIT_TAG=latest

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# How much every service writes to stdout: trace, debug, info, warn, error,
# fatal or silent. The default drops debug and trace. The field names on each
# line are a documented contract — see README.md ("Logs") before pointing a log
# pipeline at this deployment.
#
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment directory (reader#272)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The absolute path of the directory this .env and the compose file live in.
# The updater drives the stack over the docker socket, so it mounts this
# directory at the same absolute path it has on the host; that is what makes a
# relative bind like ./queues.json resolve to the same host path inside the
# updater and at a shell. The installer sets this for you. Setting it by hand
# (the manual-compose path) is required — the updater service refuses to start
# without it rather than mount the wrong path.
#
# REMIT_DEPLOY_DIR=/absolute/path/to/this/directory

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose project
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The name every container, volume and network of this deployment carries, and
# the whole of what separates two deployments on one host: each gets its own
# project, its own directory, its own loopback ports and its own .env, and they
# share nothing but the kernel, the container daemon and the images. The
# installer sets it — `install.sh --project beta` also installs that
# deployment's wrapper as `remit-beta`.
#
# Changing it on a running deployment orphans its containers and volumes: the
# names Compose looks for move with it, and the data stays behind under the old
# ones.
#
# A deployment holding other people's mail belongs on its own VM. The updater
# mounts the docker socket, so root on either stack here is root on the host.
#
# REMIT_PROJECT=remit

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Self-update (RFC 037)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# One static JSON file over HTTPS is the entire version-discovery
# mechanism: `remit update` fetches it, and installs nothing it does not
# name. No identifier and no query string are sent, and clearing this line
# turns the feature off — nothing is fetched at all.
#
# A tag existing in the registry does not mean the release finished, since
# image pushes are not atomic across the roster. The manifest is published as
# an asset of the GitHub release, uploaded only once every image is up and the
# release is flipped out of draft, which is why it is the only thing consulted.
#
# The default resolves the `stable.json` asset of the latest published,
# non-prerelease release. Point this at any HTTPS URL serving the same JSON to
# hold releases back or to run a fork.
REMIT_UPDATE_MANIFEST_URL=https://github.com/remit-mail/reader/releases/latest/download/stable.json

# How often the updater checks the manifest on its own, in seconds. The check
# only reports what is available — it never installs anything — so a shorter
# interval costs one manifest fetch and nothing else. Uncomment to override the
# six-hour default.
# REMIT_UPDATE_CHECK_INTERVAL=21600

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS termination (Caddy is the edge)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# One knob picks how Caddy serves the public origin. The deployment never
# depends on any single provider for TLS: `internal` needs nothing outside
# the box at all. PUBLIC_ORIGIN's scheme (below) must match the mode. See the
# TLS section of README.md for the trust/ports/tailnet gotchas.
#
#   off        plain HTTP on :80 (default, unchanged). Reach it over a private
#              network (tailnet, VPN, SSH tunnel).      PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://…
#   internal   HTTPS on :443 with Caddy's own CA — no external dependency,
#              works for any hostname or tailnet IP. Browsers warn until you
#              trust the root CA (README: TLS).         PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://…
#   tailscale  real Let's Encrypt cert via the local tailscaled, for this box's
#              <name>.<tailnet>.ts.net. Also set TAILSCALED_SOCKET below and
#              enable HTTPS for your tailnet.  PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://<name>.<tailnet>.ts.net
#   acme       public Let's Encrypt. Ports 80+443 reachable from the internet,
#              PUBLIC_ORIGIN's host resolvable in public DNS.  PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://…
#   tunnel     TLS terminates at a provider's edge. An outbound-only agent on
#              this box holds the connection open, so no public IP, no port
#              forward and no inbound firewall rule are needed. Also set
#              TUNNEL_TOKEN below.                    PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://…
TLS_MODE=off

# The compose services a mode needs on top of the always-on ones. Derived from
# TLS_MODE, so a mode is one edit: `tunnel` starts the tunnel agent, and the
# other four modes match no profile and start nothing extra. The optional
# profiles are separate — `remit` turns those on with a --profile flag, which
# adds to this rather than replacing it.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=${TLS_MODE:-}

# Only for TLS_MODE=tailscale: the host path of the tailscaled API socket,
# mounted into the caddy container so Caddy can fetch the certificate. Leave
# unset for every other mode (it defaults to /dev/null, which Caddy ignores).
# TAILSCALED_SOCKET=/var/run/tailscale/tailscaled.sock

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse tunnel — only for TLS_MODE=tunnel
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read this section together with "Who can create an account" below. A tunnel
# puts the sign-in and sign-up surface on the public internet: the network is no
# longer a gate, and better-auth is the only thing between a stranger and an
# account on this box.
#
# Cloudflare Tunnel is the supported edge. Create the tunnel there, point
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN's hostname at it, and route that hostname to `http://caddy:80`.
# The mapping lives at Cloudflare; nothing on the box names it.
#
# Caddy takes the client address from the header that edge overwrites on every
# request, and only from a connection on the compose network — which is what
# makes the sign-in rate limit count an attacker instead of a header they set.
#
# SECRET. The tunnel credential — it routes public traffic into this stack's
# network, so treat it like the two secrets below. `remit config` redacts it.
# Copy it out of the Cloudflare dashboard, or have install.sh read it from a
# file with --tunnel-token-file so it never reaches your shell history.
# TUNNEL_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port publishing
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which host address Caddy's two ports are published on. The defaults publish
# on every interface, which is what a deployment reached directly over a LAN,
# a tailnet or the public internet needs.
#
# A tunnel deployment sets CADDY_HTTP_BIND to a loopback address (e.g.
# 127.0.0.1:8080): the agent reaches Caddy over the compose network and needs no
# published port at all, so what is left is the operator's own route to the app
# over SSH while the tunnel is down — reachable from nowhere else. It is also
# the one host-level number two deployments on one box must not share.
# CADDY_HTTP_BIND=0.0.0.0:80
# CADDY_HTTPS_BIND=0.0.0.0:443

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public origin
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The URL your browser loads the app from — through Caddy, on this
# deployment's tailnet/VPN address or public hostname. One knob:
# docker-compose.sqlite.yml's `x-app-env` anchor derives BETTER_AUTH_URL,
# BETTER_AUTH_TRUSTED_ORIGINS, CONTENT_DELIVERY_DOMAIN, and
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS from this single value via compose variable
# interpolation, so there is nothing to keep in sync by hand. Caddy also reads
# it as its site address in every TLS mode. Set scheme://host with no trailing
# path; its scheme must match TLS_MODE above (http:// only for off).
#
# Examples: http://100.64.12.3 (a tailnet IP, off mode),
# https://remit.your-tailnet.ts.net (tailscale mode), or
# https://mail.example.com (internal or acme mode).
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://CHANGE_ME

# better-auth's own JWKS, fetched server-to-server by apisix (never crosses
# the public origin) — always the in-network backend address.
BETTER_AUTH_JWKS_URL=http://backend:8080/api/auth/jwks

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# better-auth — SECRET
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the identity JWTs every request is authenticated with. Generate
# with: openssl rand -hex 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_hex_32_min_32_chars

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who can create an account
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signup is open unless this says otherwise: unset, or any value other than
# false/0/no, lets anyone who can reach the sign-in page create an account. The
# server enforces it — a closed deployment rejects /sign-up/email outright, and
# the hidden button is presentation.
#
# On a private network that is convenient. On a public origin — TLS_MODE=tunnel
# or acme — anyone who finds the hostname can sign up. Create your own account
# first, then:
#
#   sed -i 's/^SELF_SIGN_UP_ENABLED=.*/SELF_SIGN_UP_ENABLED=false/' .env
#   remit restart
#
# Give that first account a password worth having. It is exposed to password
# spraying now, not to a LAN.
# SELF_SIGN_UP_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAP credential encryption — SECRET
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMAP/SMTP passwords and OAuth refresh tokens are envelope-encrypted
# (AES-256-GCM) before they touch the database. FAKE_KMS_KEY_ID is not a typo or
# a "demo mode" flag left on by mistake — it is the only non-AWS data-key
# provider this codebase has today (packages/secrets-service/src/service.ts):
# a real AWS KMS provider, and this one, keyed by an operator-held secret
# instead of a managed KMS key. That is exactly the env/file-class secrets
# backend RFC 034's R4 calls for at this deployment shape — the alarming
# name is a legacy of how it was first built as a KMS test double, not a
# statement about its fitness here. Treat FAKE_KMS_DATAKEY exactly like the
# other secrets on this page: generate it once, keep it safe, never commit
# it. Losing it makes every stored IMAP credential unrecoverable; rotating
# it requires re-entering every account's credentials.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
KMS_KEY_ID=FAKE_KMS_KEY_ID
FAKE_KMS_DATAKEY=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_hex_32

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage — message bodies on the filesystem backend (RFC 035 D5)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backed by the `message_storage` named volume (docker-compose.sqlite.yml). Leave
# S3_BUCKET_NAME unset — that is what selects the filesystem backend over S3
# (packages/storage-service/src/storage.ts).
STORAGE_LOCAL_PATH=/data/storage

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Semantic search — local embedding model, baked into search-index-worker
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
SEARCH_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=local
SEARCH_EMBEDDING_MODEL_ID=Xenova/paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2
SEARCH_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=384

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# All relational state is SQLite: two files on one local volume, no database
# server to run, tune, or dump. docker-compose.sqlite.yml pins DATA_BACKEND and
# both database paths itself, so this value is here for completeness rather than
# as a choice.
DATA_BACKEND=sqlite
NODE_ENV=production
NODE_NO_WARNINGS=1

# The V8 heap ceiling every Node service runs under, in megabytes. 512 fits the
# 2 vCPU / 4 GB box this deployment is sized for, with the whole roster and the
# search-index-worker's embedding model in it. Raising it lets a single service
# hold more memory before it fails — worth doing on a bigger box if indexing a
# large mailbox errors out, at the cost of the box having less headroom for
# everything else. Applies to all Node services at once; the containers have no
# hard memory limit, so a service that needs more time rather than more memory
# just runs slower.
#
# REMIT_NODE_HEAP_MB=512

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Queues (SQLite-backed SQS sidecar — the SQS-compatible seam, RFC 034)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
SQS_QUEUE_URL_MAILBOXES=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-mailboxes.fifo
SQS_QUEUE_URL_MESSAGES=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-messages.fifo
SQS_QUEUE_URL_FLAGS=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-flags.fifo
SQS_QUEUE_URL_BODY=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-body
SQS_QUEUE_URL_MAILBOX_MGMT=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-mailbox-mgmt
SQS_QUEUE_URL_MESSAGE_MGMT=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-message-mgmt
SQS_QUEUE_URL_SEARCH_INDEX=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-search-index
SQS_QUEUE_URL_SMTP=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-smtp
# The bare (unsuffixed) queue url has two live readers: it's the fallback
# remit-backend's client factory uses for the SMTP queue when
# SQS_QUEUE_URL_SMTP is unset (packages/backend/src/service/data-client.ts
# — not the case here, so dead in this deployment), and — the one that
# matters — it's what POST /accounts/{id}/sync enqueues the SYNC_MAILBOXES
# event to (packages/backend/src/service/trigger-sync.ts). Point it at
# the same mailboxes queue imap-worker polls, not a throwaway value, or
# manual/pull-to-refresh sync silently never reaches a worker.
SQS_QUEUE_URL=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-mailboxes.fifo
SQS_QUEUE_URL_ACCOUNT_FANOUT=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-account-fanout
SQS_QUEUE_URL_ACCOUNT_FINALIZE=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-account-finalize
SQS_QUEUE_URL_ACCOUNT_PURGE_DELETE=http://queue:9324/000000000000/remit-account-purge-delete.fifo

# The body-sync pause/resume toggle reads an AWS SSM parameter
# (packages/imap-worker/src/body-sync-gate.ts). There is no SSM
# emulator in this stack; the check fails open (body sync stays enabled)
# after logging a warning on every read. Harmless — this is the codebase's
# existing fail-open design, not something specific to this deployment — but
# expect a `Failed to read body-sync toggle parameter` warning in
# imap-worker's logs.
BODY_SYNC_ENABLED_PARAMETER_NAME=/self-host/Remit/bodySyncEnabled

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS SDK placeholders
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing in this stack talks to real AWS. These placeholders satisfy the
# AWS SDK v3 clients' credential resolution (SQS against the queue sidecar, SSM
# above) without needing a real account.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=local
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=local
AWS_REGION=not-a-region

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Microsoft OAuth (Entra) — optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unset by default: "Add account" works for password/app-password IMAP
# accounts without these. Set both to enable "Sign in with Microsoft" for
# IMAP account linking.
# MSOAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# MSOAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups (RFC 035 D7) — optional, off by default
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nightly encrypted database backup, shipped off-box. Only the database is
# backed up — message bodies are a cache of IMAP (the source of truth) and
# re-sync after a restore. Enable with:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.sqlite.yml --env-file .env --profile backup up -d
#
# The sidecar VACUUM INTOs the two database files (app/auth + vector), then
# encrypts and ships them with age + rclone, configured below.
#
# You own this once you turn it on: the bucket, and the age key. Losing the
# age key makes every backup unreadable; test a restore before you need one
# (see README.md).
#
# The age *public* (recipient) key. Generate a keypair with `age-keygen`;
# keep the private key offline, off this VPS, somewhere you'll still have it
# after a full disk loss — it is the only thing that decrypts a backup.
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1...
#
# Where rclone uploads to — an rclone remote name (defined by the
# RCLONE_CONFIG_<REMOTE>_* vars below) plus a bucket/path, e.g.
# "remit-backup:my-bucket/remit-pg". See
# https://rclone.org/docs/#config-file for the provider-specific variable
# names; example below is for a generic S3-compatible endpoint (Backblaze
# B2, Hetzner Object Storage, MinIO, AWS S3, ...).
# BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE=remit-backup:CHANGE_ME_bucket/remit-pg
# RCLONE_CONFIG_REMIT_BACKUP_TYPE=s3
# RCLONE_CONFIG_REMIT_BACKUP_PROVIDER=Other
# RCLONE_CONFIG_REMIT_BACKUP_ENDPOINT=CHANGE_ME
# RCLONE_CONFIG_REMIT_BACKUP_ACCESS_KEY_ID=CHANGE_ME
# RCLONE_CONFIG_REMIT_BACKUP_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=CHANGE_ME
#
# Optional overrides — defaults are a nightly backup, 30-day retention.
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail sync cadence — how often the box fetches, with nobody watching
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The `scheduler` service always runs. It is what fetches mail when no browser
# is open; everything else that triggers a sync is something a person did.
#
# MAILBOX_SYNC_OFFLINE_INTERVAL_SECONDS is the mail latency you actually get:
# an account is fetched again once its last sync is this old. Default fifteen
# minutes, which is where desktop mail clients sit. Lower it for fresher mail at
# the cost of one more IMAP connection per account per cycle; a server that rate-
# limits logins (Gmail, Fastmail) is the reason not to go far below it.
# MAILBOX_SYNC_OFFLINE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=900
#
# MAILBOX_SYNC_TICK_INTERVAL_SECONDS is how often the scheduler looks, not how
# often it fetches. It decides how late an account that has just come due is
# picked up, so keep it well below the interval above — a third of it, as the
# defaults are. Raising it above that interval means accounts are fetched on the
# tick instead, which is not what either name says.
# MAILBOX_SYNC_TICK_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300
#
# RAISE EITHER AND RAISE DOCTOR_SYNC_AGE_MAX_SECONDS WITH IT. A healthy account's
# sync age climbs to the offline interval plus one tick plus the round itself —
# about 25 minutes at the defaults — every cycle. A stall threshold below that
# peak reports accounts that are fine.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Alerts — the doctor container tells you when something breaks
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The `doctor` service always runs and always computes a verdict; `remit doctor`
# is what prints it. The two URLs below are what makes it reach you.
#
# BOTH OR NEITHER. Setting DOCTOR_WEBHOOK_URL without DOCTOR_HEARTBEAT_URL fails
# the container at startup. A webhook alone cannot tell a quiet week from a dead
# box: if the VM is off or the network is gone, nothing fires and the channel
# looks like a good day. The heartbeat URL is what notices.
#
# DOCTOR_WEBHOOK_URL is any endpoint that takes a POST — a Slack, Mattermost or
# Discord incoming webhook, an ntfy topic, your own receiver.
# DOCTOR_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/CHANGE_ME
#
# DOCTOR_HEARTBEAT_URL is pinged with a GET on every completed check. Point it at
# healthchecks.io, Cronitor or an Uptime Kuma push monitor and have that service
# alert you when the pings stop.
# DOCTOR_HEARTBEAT_URL=https://hc-ping.com/CHANGE_ME
#
# The payload is Slack-shaped JSON by default, which Mattermost and Discord also
# accept. For ntfy, or anything else that takes a raw body, set the content type
# and the default becomes plain text.
# DOCTOR_WEBHOOK_CONTENT_TYPE=text/plain
#
# Your own document, if the default does not fit. {{verdict}} is healthy or
# degraded, {{summary}} is the headline, {{reasons}} is the bullet list.
# Substituted values are escaped for the content type; in a plain-text template a
# literal \n becomes a newline, because a .env file cannot carry a real one.
# DOCTOR_WEBHOOK_TEMPLATE={"text":"{{summary}}\n{{reasons}}"}
#
# Cadence and thresholds. A verdict is announced when it changes AND has held for
# DOCTOR_DWELL_CHECKS consecutive checks — three by default, so detection is up
# to three intervals late and a flapping signal stays silent.
# DOCTOR_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
# DOCTOR_DWELL_CHECKS=3
#
# How long an account may go without completing a sync round before that counts
# as stalled. Default one hour, against a healthy peak of about 25 minutes at the
# sync cadence above — raise it if you raised either of those intervals.
# DOCTOR_SYNC_AGE_MAX_SECONDS=3600
#
# How long a worker's slowest poll loop may go without writing its heartbeat.
# Default seven minutes, the same threshold the workers' own healthcheck uses.
# DOCTOR_HEARTBEAT_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=420
#
# How long after the last IMAP/SMTP authentication failure the condition still
# counts as failing. Authentication is retried on the sync cycle, so the failures
# arrive in one burst per cycle and the quiet stretch between two bursts is not a
# recovery. Keep this above MAILBOX_SYNC_OFFLINE_INTERVAL_SECONDS, and equal to
# DOCTOR_SYNC_AGE_MAX_SECONDS for a single recovery message.
# DOCTOR_AUTH_FAILURE_HOLD_SECONDS=3600
#
# debug puts one line per check in `remit logs doctor`, saying what the check saw
# and how far the verdict has settled. Default info.
# DOCTOR_LOG_LEVEL=debug

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability profile — optional, off by default
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A log viewer (dozzle) and a metrics database with a query UI
# (VictoriaMetrics), for history on the box. Nothing here needs to be set to
# turn the profile on; enable it with:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.sqlite.yml --env-file .env --profile observability up -d
#
# Both UIs bind 127.0.0.1 and have no route through Caddy — that binding is
# what protects them, since neither asks for a password and both show data you
# would not put on the public origin. Reach them over an SSH tunnel or your
# tailnet (see README.md, "Looking at the box").
#
# The two ports below are the loopback ports the UIs answer on. Change them if
# something else on the host already has one.
# REMIT_DOZZLE_PORT=9999
# REMIT_VMUI_PORT=8428
#
# The host socket dozzle reads container logs from. The default is Docker's.
# On Podman there is no /var/run/docker.sock and the mount is refused rather
# than created, so the profile does not start until this points at the
# Docker-compatible socket (see README.md, "Podman").
# REMIT_DOZZLE_SOCKET=/run/podman/podman.sock
#
# How long metrics are kept. Default 30 days; a self-host stack's series fit in
# tens of MB over that. VictoriaMetrics durations: h d w M y. NOTE that `M` is
# months and `m` is minutes — twelve months is 12M, and 12m is rejected.
# REMIT_METRICS_RETENTION=30d
